from pathlib import Path
import csv, json, hashlib, math
from openpyxl import load_workbook

R = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
sample = R / 'Samples/Sample_idea_19 _ Hritik Mandoliya _ Machine Learning-Based Improvement of COCOMO Software Cost Estimates.xlsx'
ws = load_workbook(sample, read_only=True).active
headers = [ws.cell(1, c).value for c in range(1, 14)]
papers = [r for r in csv.DictReader((R / 'evidence/primary_studies.csv').open()) if r['Verification_Status'] != 'EXCLUDED']
registry = {r['paper_id']: r for r in csv.DictReader((R/'logs/article_registry.csv').open())}
papers.sort(key=lambda r: (-int(r['Year']), r['Exact_Title'].lower()))

# Fresh study summaries: these are not copied publisher abstracts.
abstracts = {
 'P023':'This open-access study constructs a qualitative surrogate through active instance selection, then uses it in CHC genetic feature selection with periodic original-model reevaluation. Thirteen UCI classification tasks span small and large sample sizes. A fixed-CPU-time comparison and amortized analysis distinguish setup overhead from later savings. Large-data gains coexist with small-data counterexamples. Experimental and theoretical parameter settings differ, and the crossover cardinality premise requires checking before transferring its break-even bound.',
 'P002': 'This study guides genetic feature selection with a comparatively inexpensive correlation criterion before evaluating candidate subsets with k-nearest neighbours. Correlation information influences crossover and mutation, aiming to spend classifier evaluations on more promising masks. Five repeated runs and several benchmark datasets support within-study comparisons. Computational efficiency is evaluated through time needed to attain a specified score. Comparator times use the minimum across runs, while the proposed method uses a mean, so the timing results require qualified interpretation. Its LSVT comparison also provides a counterexample to universal performance or speed superiority.',
 'P003': 'The proposed hybrid uses feature pools derived from several ranking methods to guide an NSGA-II search. A linear support vector machine evaluates macro-F1, while feature count supplies the competing objective. The study examines high-dimensional classification and repeated searches across a constrained subset-size range. Reported scores aggregate evaluations over feature counts and executions rather than describing one final deployment mask. The authors identify execution time as a principal limitation. The timing comparison combines several baseline methods, and inconsistencies between dataset and timing-table labels warrant verification before quantitative reuse.',
 'P004': 'This study extends genetic feature selection to multi-view classification by searching both within individual views and between views. Binary feature masks and view-level representations operate within a multi-niche NSGA-II framework. Experiments cover synthetic data, cancer datasets, and ADNI/TADPOLE tasks, including external evaluation sets. The results distinguish classification performance from recovery of known informative synthetic features. Reported advantages depend on the task and metric; ADNI specificity includes a tie with Lasso-SVM. Training-time details are deferred to supplementary material that has not been verified in this project.',
 'P005': 'The method modifies population initialization, crossover, and parent selection to guide genetic feature search. Feature relationships are organized through a cosine-similarity construction and clustering, while the objective rewards classification accuracy and dimensionality reduction. Its experimental comparisons include repeated runs and statistical testing. Because several components change together, the results establish the behaviour of the combined method rather than the isolated effect of each operator. The inspected main text does not clearly specify an untouched outer evaluation procedure or measured runtime and hardware, limiting claims about generalization and computational efficiency.',
 'P006': 'The method first combines feature rankings from classification-error impurity, mutual information, and Fisher ratio, then applies an adaptive genetic algorithm supported by an external repository. Classification accuracy supplies the wrapper fitness, and variable-length subsets support compact solutions. Biomedical benchmarks show improvements over an all-feature decision tree, alongside comparator results that prevent universal superiority claims. The paper reports fitness-evaluation counts instead of measured wall-clock time. Ambiguity about validation nesting and an inconsistency in repeat counts require care when interpreting its performance and computational evidence.',
 'P010': 'The study builds a graph from feature similarities, groups variables through community detection, and repairs genetic subsets using those groups. Classification and redundancy information enter a scalar fitness expression. Comparisons include several wrappers and classifiers on standard and high-dimensional datasets. Accuracy advantages vary by dataset, and selected-feature counts are means across runs. The paper explicitly measures feature-selection time separately from final classification time. Its description places normalization before data partitioning, creating a preprocessing-boundary concern. The number of datasets stated in the abstract also differs from the principal benchmark tables.',
 'P012': 'TAGA combines Fisher filtering, an asexual genetic search, a long-term tabu list, and sequential-forward guidance across subset cardinalities. Integer permutations represent selected and unselected feature IDs, and a minimum-redundancy maximum-relevance criterion evaluates subsets without embedding a classifier in the search fitness. Several classifiers subsequently assess the selected subsets. Results and runtime advantages depend on the dataset and classifier. Evidence here comes from the repository-designated accepted manuscript. Its benchmark settings differ from the illustrative author code, and the thesis and preprint must not be treated as additional independent studies.',
 'P013': 'MOEA-ISa addresses large-scale classification feature selection through interval-based initialization and self-adaptive crossover. Binary masks are evaluated for classification error and feature count, producing a Pareto set rather than a single universally preferred subset. Experiments use explicit training/test partitions, repeated searches, and a fixed evaluation budget. Hypervolume and inverted generational distance assess the quality of the returned fronts; these quantities are not classification accuracy. ReliefF guidance and bookkeeping add computation beyond classifier evaluations. A deployment comparison would also need a declared training-side rule for choosing one final mask.',
 'P014': 'FTGGA guides binary feature search using ReliefF and evolving feature thresholds. Its multi-objective feature-scoring mechanism should be distinguished from Pareto optimization of complete chromosomes: chromosome fitness minimizes classification error. The study compares the method on gene-expression datasets using repeated runs, subset sizes, accuracy, and measured runtime. Competitive predictive results coexist with a runtime disadvantage and dataset-specific counterexamples. Its dual-population design is reflected in comparator population sizes, but equal population sizes alone do not account for every initialization and guidance cost. Independent outer validation remains insufficiently explicit.',
 'P016': 'This industrial-control intrusion-detection study combines rank information and a guided genetic feature search, evaluating the resulting subsets on SWaT and WADI data. Classification accuracy, weighted F1, and feature relationships contribute to the reported analysis. The method produces compact subsets in comparisons with conventional feature selectors and optimization methods. Its traditional-selector cross-validation table and optimization-comparison table report different SWaT accuracy values, which must remain separate. Reductions in subsequent classifier computation do not establish lower total genetic-search cost. Temporal independence, preprocessing placement, and independent search repetitions require further verification.',
 'P017': 'DIDA combines dominance-based feature guidance, genetic search, and analysis of duplicate solutions for multi-objective feature selection. Binary subsets are evaluated under classification-error and feature-ratio objectives. The study uses explicit training/test partitions, repeated searches, and an evaluation ceiling linked to population size. Hypervolume and inverted generational distance characterize front quality, while minimum classification error targets a different aspect of the returned solutions. The empirical reference front and final deployment choice affect interpretation. Fitness-evaluation matching does not establish equal wall-clock cost, especially when duplicate analysis adds bookkeeping.',
 'P018': 'The SLI–GA hybrid ranks features using sorted-label interference, then mixes rank-guided and random initialization in a binary genetic wrapper. Experiments distinguish filter evaluation from wrapper evaluation and use k-nearest-neighbour or neural-network scoring. The full journal text reports substantial feature reduction and lower classifier error, but the neural-network settings specify mean-squared error while a comparative table expresses accuracy as one minus error. Discrete prediction-based accuracy therefore needs independent confirmation. Dataset names and a population-size column are also ambiguous. Evidence is extracted from full journal XML, with section and table locators rather than unavailable PDF pages.',
 'P021': 'This study evaluates genetic feature selection for an intrusion detector implemented with XGBoost, including inference on a Jetson Nano. Binary subsets have prescribed cardinalities, allowing comparisons between feature count, classification performance, and embedded computation. The paper separates a substantial offline search cost from inference on a reference test batch. Batch timing should not be interpreted as per-packet latency, and real-time capture was not evaluated. Its evidence therefore supports a specific deployment-cost comparison rather than a general real-time detection claim. Independent repetitions and validation nesting are not sufficiently clear in the inspected text.',
 'P022': 'The method searches packet-header feature subsets with a binary genetic algorithm and compares several classifiers for network-incident identification. A three-batch evaluation procedure distinguishes subset selection from later testing. The objective balances a predictive score with subset compactness. Its results section states that the reported accuracy denotes F1, so the numerical labels must be interpreted carefully. Random packet partitions and duplicate-vector checks do not establish independence between sessions or capture environments. The public implementation supports inspection, but measured runtime and hardware details are not fully reported in the paper.',
 'P001': 'This record concerns adaptive crossover in multi-objective binary genetic feature selection for classification. The exact title, authors, journal, year, and DOI have been checked. Full text remains unavailable through the legitimate routes examined. Detailed operators, validation boundaries, datasets, Pareto evaluation, repeat design, and computational results have not been verified. The record is retained for coverage tracking and contributes no numerical findings to the full-text synthesis.',
 'P007': 'The accessible institutional abstract describes a genetic feature-selection method for intrusion detection, including parameter tuning and classifier comparisons on CIRA-CIC-DOHBrw-2020, UNSW-NB15, and Bot-IoT. The publication identity is verified, but the institutional record provides a subscription link rather than a full manuscript. Detailed representation, fitness, operators, split design, runtime, and result tables remain unverified. Abstract-level performance statements are not treated as independently checked full-text results.',
 'P008': 'This verified publication record identifies a problem-specific non-dominated sorting genetic algorithm for supervised feature selection. The full manuscript has not been obtained. Its precise objective definitions, operator implementation, evaluation budget, validation procedure, and final-subset selection rule therefore remain unverified. No numerical results are inferred from the title or secondary surveys. The record remains separate from the included full-text empirical evidence.',
 'P011': 'The publisher abstract describes GeFeS as a parallel genetic wrapper with feature weighting, modified operators, and nested cross-validation. Publication metadata identifies a classification feature-selection study in Computers in Biology and Medicine. The complete methods and result tables remain inaccessible through the legitimate routes tried. Exact nesting, implementation settings, repetitions, and parallel timing cannot be certified from the abstract. This candidate is retained without adding unverified numerical findings to the review.',
 'P015': 'The publisher abstract frames feature selection for early time-series classification through three objectives: classification performance, the time at which classification can begin, and classification execution time. These objectives distinguish earliness from genetic-search runtime. Full text remains purchase-only through the student’s available access. Dataset details, objective implementation, validation, repeat counts, evaluation budgets, and experimental tables are not verified. The abstract supports the problem scope only, and no purchase is required for this project.',
 'P019': 'This verified record concerns rival and fast rival genetic algorithms for classification feature selection. The full journal manuscript remains unavailable. The author-linked public implementation is a relevant lead, but it cannot substitute for the paper’s full experimental protocol and tables. Representation, operator settings, datasets, validation, runtime boundaries, and numerical results are not yet fully verified. The record is included in the literature catalogue rather than the full-text comparative synthesis.'
}

